course: world
lesson: 57
title: Caring for the environment
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The environment is everything around us: air, water, soil, plants and animals. All of it is linked, so harm in one place spreads to others.
explain: Pollution is putting harmful things into the environment. Smoke from factories and cars dirties the air, waste and chemicals dirty rivers and seas, and litter harms the land and the animals that live on it.
explain: Plastic is a big problem because it takes hundreds of years to break down. A plastic bag in the sea can be mistaken for a jellyfish and swallowed by a turtle.
explain: We can help by the three Rs: Reduce (use less), Reuse (use things again) and Recycle (turn old materials into new things). Recycling an aluminium can saves most of the energy needed to make a new one.
explain: Protecting nature means keeping homes for wild animals — forests, wetlands, meadows and seas — because when a habitat is destroyed, the animals living there lose their food and shelter.
fact: Pollution is harmful material put into air, water or soil.
fact: Smoke from vehicles, factories and burning rubbish pollutes the air.
fact: Plastic can take hundreds of years to break down.
fact: Sea animals can swallow plastic or get tangled in it.
fact: Reduce, Reuse and Recycle are three ways to make less waste.
fact: Reducing waste helps most, because the waste is never made.
fact: Paper, glass, metal cans and many plastics can be recycled.
fact: Food scraps and leaves can be turned into compost that feeds the soil.
fact: A habitat is the natural home of a plant or an animal.
fact: Cutting down forests destroys habitats and removes trees that clean the air by taking in carbon dioxide.
fact: Nature reserves and national parks protect wild plants and animals.
exercise: What is pollution? | Harmful things put into nature | Rain falling on the roofs of a big city | Plants growing wild in a farmer's field | Animals moving to a new home in spring
exercise: Why is plastic litter such a problem? | It takes hundreds of years to break down | It breaks down in a few days in the rain | It feeds sea animals well | It turns into clean water
exercise: What does "reduce" mean in the three Rs? | Using less in the first place | Throwing things away more often | Burning old things | Making things bigger
exercise: Which is an example of reusing? | Filling an old jam jar with pencils | Throwing a jam jar into the bin | Melting a jar down into brand new glass | Buying a new jar each week
exercise: What happens to cans that are recycled? | They are melted into new metal | They are buried and left forever | They are burned to keep houses warm | They are thrown far out into the sea
exercise: Why is reducing waste even better than recycling it? | The waste is never made, so no energy is spent on it | Recycling makes more rubbish than it saves | Reducing creates new materials | Recycled things cannot be used again
exercise: Which of these can be turned into compost? | Apple cores and fallen leaves | Plastic bottles | Glass jars | Metal cans
exercise: Why can a plastic bag in the sea harm a turtle? | The turtle may swallow it, thinking it is a jellyfish | The bag makes the water too warm | The bag pulls turtles with magnetism | Turtles eat only plastic
exercise: What is a habitat? | The natural home of a plant or animal | A cage in a zoo where animals are kept safe | A kind of pollution | A machine for recycling
exercise: What happens to animals when their forest is cut down? | They lose their food and shelter | They are not affected by it at all | They get more food than they had before | They grow taller to reach the last trees
exercise: Which helps keep the air clean? | Cycling or walking instead of driving | Burning all the rubbish in the garden at night | Leaving car engines running | Cutting down more trees
exercise: What are national parks and nature reserves for? | Protecting wild plants and animals | Building new factories where it is quiet | Storing rubbish | Testing new cars
exercise: Which of these pollutes a river? | Pouring oil and paint down the drain | Planting a row of trees along the bank | Picking up litter that lies near the water | Building a little wooden home for ducks
test: A family starts buying loose fruit instead of fruit packed in plastic trays. Which of the three Rs is this? | Reduce | Reuse | Recycle | Repair
test: Why is it a good idea to recycle aluminium cans rather than make new ones? | Recycling saves most of the energy needed to make new metal | New cans are made of plastic | Recycled cans are heavier and stronger | It makes cans break down faster
test: A family sorts its rubbish. Where should a newspaper go? | Into the paper recycling | Into the compost heap with meat | Into the river | Onto a bonfire
test: Litter on a beach is carried away by the tide. What could happen next? | Sea animals may swallow it or get caught in it | It disappears completely within a single day or two | It turns into sand at once | It becomes food for plants
test: Which is true about plastic? | Most plastic lasts for hundreds of years | Plastic breaks down quickly in the sea, in a few weeks | Plastic is made from fresh fruit | Plastic cannot be recycled at all
test: A wetland where many birds nest is drained to build a car park. What is the main harm? | The birds lose their habitat | The car park will be too wet | The birds get more food | The air becomes cleaner
test: Why does cutting down large forests harm the air? | Fewer trees are left to take in carbon dioxide | Trees make the air dirty | Forests stop the wind | Cut trees release clean water that rains on cities
test: What is the best thing to do with a sandwich wrapper if there is no bin nearby? | Carry it home and put it in a bin | Drop it on the grass for the birds | Throw it in a pond where nobody sees | Hide it under a bush so it is out of sight
test: why's plastic in the sea such a big deal | Sea animals swallow it or get tangled, and it lasts for hundreds of years | Plastic makes the water too salty | Plastic melts into food for fish | It only looks ugly on beaches
test: chuck the apple core in the bin or what | Put it in compost to feed the soil | Throw it in the sea | Burn it on a bonfire along with plastic | Bury it in plastic bags
test: my sister says recycling is the best thing you can do about waste. true? | No, reducing helps most because the waste is never made | Yes, recycling beats everything | Yes, reducing does nothing at all | No, throwing away is best
test: Jack reckons cutting forests only loses wood. right? | No, it destroys habitats and removes trees that clean the air | Yes, wood is all we lose | Yes, animals do not live in forests | No, it makes more space for animals
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises pollution? | Harmful material introduced into the air, water or soil | Any material which is subsequently recycled | The natural decomposition of fallen leaves | The planting of trees upon cleared land
exam: Identify, among the practices of Reduce, Reuse and Recycle, that which is of the greatest benefit, together with the reason therefor. | Reduce, since the waste is never produced | Recycle, since every material may be remade | Reuse, since objects are thereby destroyed | Recycle, since it requires no energy
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the period required for the decomposition of plastic. | It may require hundreds of years | It is accomplished within a few days | It is accomplished within a single season | It never commences under any conditions
exam: It is given that a forest is felled in its entirety. Determine the principal consequence for the wild creatures resident therein. | Their habitat is destroyed | Their habitat is enlarged | Their food supply is increased | Their number is necessarily augmented
